## std.concurrent.atomic — atomic operations (docs/22)
##
## The four primitives — load, store, fetch_add, compare_exchange —
## are COMPILER INTRINSICS: the builtin router (src/codegen/builtins.zig)
## intercepts calls by name+arity and emits the hardware instruction
## (`@atomicLoad` / `@atomicStore` / `@atomicRmw` / `@cmpxchgStrong`,
## all seq_cst) directly at the call site. No import is needed; this
## module exists so the surface has a documented home and imports
## RESOLVE (whole-module `import std.concurrent.atomic` binds these
## names via alias constants, and zig analyzes imported aliases' fns —
## so the bodies must COMPILE, never execute).
##
## Body note: the router is the only caller — no zag call site ever
## reaches these bodies. They discard their params and return a zero
## value purely to satisfy zig's unused-parameter / missing-return
## checks when the import aliases force body analysis. (`return
## unreachable;` was tried and rejected: zig 0.16 flags
## `return unreachable` as unreachable code, and plain stubs trip the
## unused-parameter check.)
##
## Signatures:
##
##   load(ptr)              -> T        @atomicLoad(T, ptr, .seq_cst)
##   store(ptr, val)        void        @atomicStore(T, ptr, val, .seq_cst)
##   fetch_add(ptr, val)    -> T        @atomicRmw(T, ptr, .Add, val, .seq_cst)
##   compare_exchange(p,o,n) -> bool    (@cmpxchgStrong(T, p, o, n, ...) == null)
##
## compare_exchange returns the CAS SUCCESS flag (true = swapped).
## zig's raw cmpxchg returns `?T` (null on success, the witnessed
## value on failure); the intrinsic wraps it with `== null` so the
## bool is nameable in zag — lock loops build directly on it (see
## std.concurrent.mutex.lock).
##
## Type note: the *i32 annotations are the load-bearing minimum —
## the router emits `@TypeOf(ptr.*)` at every site, so any
## integer-pointer type works at the hardware level. The decls
## below keep i32 because the router fires on name+arity before
## these signatures are ever consulted.

pub fun load(ptr: *i32) -> i32 {
    _ = ptr;
    return 0;
}
pub fun store(ptr: *i32, val: i32) {
    _ = ptr;
    _ = val;
}
pub fun fetch_add(ptr: *i32, val: i32) -> i32 {
    _ = ptr;
    _ = val;
    return 0;
}
pub fun compare_exchange(ptr: *i32, expected: i32, replacement: i32) -> bool {
    _ = ptr;
    _ = expected;
    _ = replacement;
    return false;
}
